---
/**
 * 404.
 *
 * Astro emits this as `dist/404.html` and Cloudflare Pages serves it, with a 404
 * status, for any path that matches no file and no rule in `public/_redirects`.
 * Nothing needs configuring for that; deleting this file is what would change it,
 * and the fallback is Cloudflare's own white page.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT IS ON IT, AND WHY IT IS BUILT OUT OF PARTS THAT ALREADY EXIST
 *
 * One light page, the roof over the message, and the wood under it.
 *
 *   1. The roof from the logo — `RoofMark.astro`, the vector recreation, not the
 *      raster lockup — set wide, with the number and the copy tucked UNDER it.
 *      The number rises into the triangle of empty space beneath the apex, so the
 *      block reads as standing under a roof rather than beside a picture of one.
 *      That is also the logo's own construction: mark above, words under it.
 *   2. No panel colour. The page is the site's ordinary `--omitka` ground, so
 *      there is one continuous surface from the header to the trees.
 *   3. The FOOTER'S OWN treeline rises into the bottom of it, in `--les`, exactly
 *      as it does on every other page. Nothing was added to draw it. The section
 *      is sized so the wood is IN THE FIRST SCREEN with the message — see the
 *      `min-height` and the bottom padding, which are a pair.
 *
 * ⚠ TWO THINGS THAT WERE TRIED AND ARE NOT COMING BACK
 *
 *   - A `--les` panel behind the message, so the white raster logo could be used.
 *     It cut the page in half and hid the wood below the fold. The vector roof
 *     takes `currentColor` and needs no dark ground, which is what unlocked this
 *     version.
 *   - The same treeline recoloured to `--kamen` and hung along the bottom of that
 *     panel, facing the footer's across a clearing. It reads as a bank of dark
 *     spikes hanging downward rather than as trees — the reason is written at the
 *     top of Treeline.astro. It does not invert.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The two links are the two that answer the question the reader actually has.
 * The rest of the site — /chalupa, /terminy-a-ceny, /okoli, /galerie, /kontakt —
 * is one scroll further down in the footer's Rozcestník, which is on this page
 * like every other, so repeating it here would be the same list twice. The phone
 * number is on it because a guest who cannot find a page can still ring.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RoofMark from '../components/decor/RoofMark.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const NBSP = ' ';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Stránka nenalezena"
  description="Tuhle stránku jsme nenašli. Vraťte se na úvod chalupy Heřmanka, nebo nám napište a my poradíme."
  noindex
  headerSolid
>
  <section class="ztraceno" aria-labelledby="ztraceno-nadpis">
    <div class="wrap ztraceno-inner">
      {/*
        Decorative: the site name is already in the header, the title and the
        footer. `width` here is only the SVG's intrinsic attribute — the CSS
        below overrides it, and the viewBox does the scaling.
      */}
      <RoofMark width={560} class="ztraceno-strecha" />

      {/*
        Also decorative. The heading under it says the same thing in words, and a
        screen reader announcing "čtyři sta čtyři" first helps nobody.
      */}
      <p class="ztraceno-cislo" aria-hidden="true">404</p>

      <h1 id="ztraceno-nadpis">Tudy cesta nevede</h1>

      <p class="ztraceno-lead">
        Stránka, kterou hledáte, tady není{NBSP}— nejspíš se přestěhovala, nebo
        v{NBSP}odkazu chybí písmenko.
      </p>

      <div class="ztraceno-akce">
        <a href="/" class="btn btn-primary">Zpět na úvod</a>
        {/* Absolute, and /kontakt rather than /#poptavka — the same target the
            footer's CTA uses, for the reason written beside it there. */}
        <a href="/kontakt#poptavka" class="btn btn-secondary">Napsat nám</a>
      </div>

      <p class="ztraceno-tel">
        Nebo zavolejte <a href={`tel:${SITE.telHref}`}>{SITE.telefon}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

{/*
  ⚠ THE ONE GLOBAL RULE ON THIS PAGE, AND IT IS DELIBERATE.

  The footer's wood is sized for a page that has just scrolled through eleven
  sections: `--treeline-h` is up to 460px, and `main > *:last-child` in
  global.css reserves 68% of that as clear ground above it. On a 404 — one short
  block and then the footer — that reserved band is most of the screen, and the
  message ends up stranded above a field of nothing.

  Both numbers come from the SAME variable, so overriding it here scales the wood
  and its clearance together and nothing else on the page has to know. `is:global`
  because a scoped style cannot reach `:root`, and page-level styles ship with
  this page alone — verified: `dist/index.html` carries no trace of it.

  Unlayered, so it beats global.css's own `:root` inside `@layer components`.

  ⚠ THERE IS A FLOOR ON IT, AND IT IS GEOMETRY, NOT TASTE.

  The artwork is 1440 × 332 and it is drawn `xMidYMax slice` — scaled to COVER
  the box, cropping whatever does not fit. Which edge gets cropped depends
  entirely on the box's own ratio:

      box height / box width  >=  332 / 1440  =  0.2306   →  the SIDES crop
      box height / box width  <   0.2306                  →  the TOP crops

  Cropping the sides is what the drawing is designed for: one stretch of wood
  looks like any other, and the page simply shows less of it. Cropping the top
  takes the tips off the tallest trees and leaves them standing flat — and it
  shows first at the LEFT AND RIGHT EDGES, because the saddle (forest.ts) is what
  puts the full-height trees there. A `clamp(150px, 14vw, 200px)` version of this
  line sat at 0.14 and was slicing 132px off the top at 1440.

  `24vw` holds the ratio at 0.24 — just clear of the floor — at every width, which
  is why it is a plain viewport unit and not a clamp. The 190px minimum only
  bites on a phone, where it makes the ratio LARGER, not smaller, so it is safe.

  It is also the page's colour balance: the wood is a solid `--les` mass whose
  feet merge into the footer with no seam, so every 100px here is 100px of the
  white page turning dark green. 24vw is the smallest it can be without cutting
  the trees, which makes this the whole of the trade-off, already made.
*/}
<style is:global>
  :root {
    --treeline-h: max(190px, 24vw);
  }
</style>

<style>
  /*
   * The section is sized so that the message AND the top of the wood are on the
   * first screen together — that is the whole layout, and the two rules below
   * are what buy it:
   *
   *   `min-height: 100svh` makes the section fill the viewport, and
   *   `align-content: end` drops the content to the BOTTOM of it, right above
   *   the trees, instead of floating in the middle of an empty page.
   *
   * ⚠ The bottom padding is what the content stands clear of, and it is bigger
   * than the global clearance rule's. That rule reserves 68% of the wood, which
   * is all the saddle reaches under the middle of a normal page; here there is
   * nothing else on the page, so the wood gets 80% and its tallest edge trees
   * have somewhere to be. Cut it and the copy lands in the canopy.
   *
   * ⚠ EVERY SIZE BELOW IS PART OF A HEIGHT BUDGET. `min-height` only holds the
   * floor — once the content plus these two paddings is taller than the viewport,
   * the section grows and the wood goes back below the fold, which is the one
   * thing this layout exists to prevent.
   *
   * So the three big items — this padding, the mark and the number — are all
   * sized in `svh` as well as in `rem`, and a short laptop window shrinks the lot
   * rather than pushing the trees off the bottom. A fixed 8rem/34rem/5.5rem
   * version of this block put the whole wood below the fold at 1024 × 720.
   * Measure at that size before enlarging any of them.
   *
   * `align-content: center` rather than `end`. Bottom-anchoring the block was
   * right while the wood was a 200px fringe; at its proper height there is no
   * longer enough screen for both, and anchoring to the bottom spent every spare
   * pixel on the gap above the copy — 29px of air over the roof at 1280 × 800.
   * Centred, the slack is split, and the roof keeps its air on every screen.
   *
   * ⚠ The bottom padding is measured against the wood, not picked. The ranks are
   * generated with a saddle (forest.ts), so under the MIDDLE of the page — the
   * only part the copy is ever over — the canopy reaches about 0.42 of the wood's
   * height; the tall edge trees stand well outside a 40rem measure. 0.5 clears
   * that with air to spare. Below about 0.45 the last line starts meeting
   * branches.
   */
  .ztraceno {
    display: grid;
    align-content: center;
    min-height: 100svh;
    /*
     * ⚠ The 9rem MINIMUM is the air above the roof, and it is a floor rather than
     * a scaled value on purpose.
     *
     * At the wood's proper height there is no longer a screen tall enough for
     * generous air AND the whole wood at 1280 × 800 — one of them has to give.
     * This gives the air and lets a short window scroll by 25–50px, so the
     * canopy is always in the first screen and the trunks may not be. The other
     * way round — everything on screen, 22px over the roof — is what this
     * replaced.
     */
    padding-block: clamp(9rem, 14svh, 11rem) calc(var(--treeline-h) * 0.5 + 1.5rem);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .ztraceno-inner {
    max-width: 40rem;
  }

  .ztraceno-inner > * {
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* ---- Under the roof ----------------------------------------------------
   *
   * The mark is drawn WIDER than everything beneath it. That is the only reason
   * the arrangement reads as shelter: a roof narrower than the text it covers is
   * just an ornament sitting on top of a paragraph.
   *
   * `--les`, the same weight as the body type. Brass was tried and the mark
   * turned into a badge — it is architecture here, not a decorative rule.
   */
  .ztraceno-strecha {
    display: block;
    /* The third term is the height budget above — 40svh of WIDTH, which at this
       mark's 510:256 works out at about a fifth of the window's height. */
    width: min(22rem, 72%, 40svh);
    /*
     * ⚠ `height: auto` ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH ON AN INLINE <svg>.
     *
     * RoofMark emits `width` and `height` attributes, and an inline SVG is not
     * treated like an <img>: `auto` resolves to that height ATTRIBUTE, not to the
     * viewBox's ratio. So the box stayed 281px tall while CSS narrowed it, the
     * drawing letterboxed inside it under the default `xMidYMid meet`, and the
     * mark sat in 136px of invisible padding — which is exactly the height budget
     * this layout is fighting for. The ratio is the viewBox's own.
     */
    aspect-ratio: 510 / 256;
    height: auto;
    color: var(--color-les);
  }

  /*
   * ⚠ The negative margin is the point of the whole block, not a nudge.
   *
   * The mark's two slopes leave a triangle of empty space under the apex, and
   * the number is pulled up INTO it — the same tuck the logo itself does with
   * its wordmark. Take it out and the roof and the number are two separate
   * things stacked in a column.
   *
   * It is in `em` so it tracks the number's own size through the clamp. Anything
   * past about -0.3em and the digits foul the underside of the slopes.
   */
  .ztraceno-cislo {
    margin-top: -0.22em;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 6svh, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    /* --mosaz-tmava on --omitka = 6.05:1. Brass belongs on the number, which is
       the one thing here that is not architecture and not a sentence. */
    color: var(--color-mosaz-tmava);
  }

  h1 {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .ztraceno-lead {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    max-width: 34ch;
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
    color: var(--color-kura);
  }

  .ztraceno-akce {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .ztraceno-tel {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    color: var(--color-kura);
  }

  .ztraceno-tel a {
    color: var(--color-mosaz-tmava);
    text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
  }

  @media (width < 25rem) {
    .ztraceno-akce .btn {
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
